<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Egypt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> is the southern part of Egypt, the narrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> stretching from around Memphis southwards to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-cataract">First Cataract</a> at Aswan. It was home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, the religious centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. By around 1100 BCE the High Priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> governed Upper Egypt as virtually independent rulers, and the region later came under the control of the Kushite kings of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-egypt.jpg" alt="Upper Egypt" width="216" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Upper_Egypt_Nomes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley in the south of the country. Today it is counted from Beni Suef to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-cataract">First Cataract</a>, which precedes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>, the lake formed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a>. Its northern part, between Sohag and El-Ayait, is also known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a>.</p><p>In ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> it was called tꜣ šmꜣw, the Land of Reeds or Sedgeland, after the sedges that grow there. The Arabic name Sa'id means uplands, from a root meaning to go up. Its people are known as Sa'idis and generally speak Sa'idi Egyptian Arabic. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biblical-hebrew">Biblical Hebrew</a> and Akkadian it was known by names that come from the Egyptian for the southern land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By about 3600 BCE Neolithic societies along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> based their culture on crops and the domestication of animals. A new pottery appeared, related to Levantine ceramics, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> implements became common. The towns of Upper Egypt then politically unified, as did those of the Delta, which led to warfare between the two new kingdoms. The main city of prehistoric Upper Egypt was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekhen">Nekhen</a>, and its patron deity was the goddess Nekhbet, depicted as a vulture.</p><p>During his reign in Upper Egypt King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a> defeated his enemies in the Delta and became sole ruler of the two lands. It is believed that the rulers of the supposed Thinite Confederacy had united Upper Egypt by absorbing rival city states during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqada-iii">Naqada III</a> period, and its unification with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> ushered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>. In royal symbolism Upper Egypt was represented by the tall White Crown, the flowering lotus and the sedge, and after unification its patron Nekhbet was shown with the patron of Lower Egypt as the Two Ladies.</p><p>For most of ancient history <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> was its administrative centre. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> devastated it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s importance declined, and under the Ptolemies Ptolemais Hermiou became the capital of Upper Egypt. In the eleventh century large numbers of pastoralists, known as Hilalians, left Upper Egypt for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, probably because grazing conditions had worsened as the Medieval Warm Period began.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> now forms seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a>: Beni Suef, Minya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asyut">Asyut</a>, Sohag, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qena">Qena</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a> and Aswan. Its large cities include Asyut, Luxor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a> and Aswan. In twentieth-century Egypt the title Prince of the Sa'id, meaning Prince of Upper Egypt, was used by the heir apparent to the Egyptian throne.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Upper Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He established Islam as the religion of the state, encouraged the immigration of literate scholars and merchants from Bornu and Bagirmi, and developed the Darb al-Arba'in or Darb el-Arba'in (the “Forty Days Road”), the desert trade route linking Darfur's commercial centre at Kobbei to Asyut in <b>Upper Egypt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By approximately 1080 BCE, the High Priest of Amun Herihor governed <b>Upper Egypt</b> as a de facto independent ruler, while Smendes controlled Lower Egypt from Tanis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the History of the Patriarchs of Alexandria, King Kyriakos marched into <b>Upper Egypt</b> around 747 to demand the release of the imprisoned Coptic patriarch, and Arab historians report that Nubian forces raided Upper Egypt as far as Akhmim in the 960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>This broadened the military base, but it also shifted the balance of power within society, and the great native revolt in <b>Upper Egypt</b> (206–186 BCE) followed soon afterwards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nag Hammadi library, found near Nag Hammadi in <b>Upper Egypt</b> in 1945, holds twelve papyrus codices with fifty-two mostly Gnostic treatises, three works from the Corpus Hermeticum and a partial translation of Plato's Republic.</blockquote><a href="/religion/gnosticism">Gnosticism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…641 and 642, Arabic gradually replaced it: Arabic became the language of administration in 706, Coptic Christians increasingly spoke and wrote Arabic from the 10th century, and Coptic seems to have died out as a spoken language, last in villages of <b>Upper Egypt</b>, by about the 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
